  "query_preview": "..\nThe upper pattern this afternoon is characterized by a strong \nupper-low centered across southeast California and Hurricane \nPolo (a category 2 hurricane with maximum sustained winds of 75\nmph near 28.0N/110.6W or over east-central Sonora Mexico, \ntracking NE at 15 kts). Polo remains forecast to rapidly weaken\nas it continues to weaken and accelerate toward the NE tonight,\nbecoming a tropical storm over far west-cenral Chihuahua by 5 \npm today.\n\nAs of this writing, a band of training moderate to strong \nrain bands and embedded thunderstorms extends from Nogales, \nnorth-northeast to western Greenlee county, with area \nrivers/washes at Minor or Moderate flood stages, including the \nGila, Brawley, Aravaipa Canyon and other locations including \nSabino Creek and a low water crossing between mile marker 374\nand 375 along highway 70 west of Duncan.\n\nThe band results from a merger between that from the upper low \ncold frontal band and convective bands associated with the \ntropical storm moving into Chihuahua, Mexico. This will slowly\ntranslate east-northeastward across Cochise county, with several\nCAM's continuing to depict at least two additional rounds of \nshower activity overnight which are focused into bands. The\nfirst should rotate through near midnight, while the second\nmoves through through during the pre-dawn hours. CA upper low\ncenter continues to exhibit a southerly component to its track,\nallowing core convection to bring the additional rounds of\nshowers overnight for us.  \n\nFlow aloft will turn from northwest later tomorrow, allowing\ndrier air advection into the region and a rebound in high \ntemperatures to near normal under sunny to mostly sunny skies\nthrough at least weeks end. NBM medium and extended range\nforecasts hint at back door cold frontal development to our east\nconcurrent with redevelopment or weak upper low over southeast \nNM. Low development would reinforce the gradient driven \nsoutheast winds along the Gila river and adjacent valleys, along\nwith subtle moisture advection and afternoon convection.",
